%%if postgresql%%
Database-enabled applications include `infrastructure::audit::PgAuditSink`,
`infrastructure::auth::PostgresSessionStore`, and
`infrastructure::tenancy::PgMembershipStore`. The runtime role can append
structured security-audit events but cannot read, update, or delete audit
history.

%%end%%
Create a reversible application migration with:

```bash
forge generate migration create_customers
```

This creates matching `.up.sql` and `.down.sql` files in `migrations/`.
Migration SQL is checked out with LF line endings so SQLx hashes remain stable
across operating systems.
%%if views%%

Generate an HTML CRUD resource (migration, entity, use cases, repository,
handlers, views and tests) with:

```bash
forge generate scaffold post title:string body:text published:boolean --global
```

The generator only creates files; it prints the `pub mod` lines and the
`compose` statements (in `src/bootstrap/mod.rs`) that wire the resource. Its
routes answer `403` until you choose who may use them in
`src/adapters/http/access.rs`.

Add `--broadcast` for live updates: open index pages apply every create,
update and delete over Server-Sent Events (Turbo Streams). Production needs
the stream signing key, the same on every replica:

```bash
forge generate scaffold event name:string starts_at:timestamp --tenant-scoped --broadcast
export FORGE_STREAMS_KEY=$(openssl rand -base64 32)
```

That is why `Cargo.toml` enables the `broadcast` feature of `forge`: it only
compiles the live-update code, and nothing is served, connected or started
until you add the printed `Streams::register` wiring. If this application
will never broadcast, you may remove `"broadcast"` from the features.
%%end%%
%%if authentication%%

### Sign in

The application has sign-in, sign-out and password-reset pages
(`src/adapters/http/authentication.rs`, `templates/sessions/`,
`templates/passwords/`), like `rails generate authentication`. Create the
first tenant and its owner with the migration role, then sign in:

```bash
export FORGE_MIGRATION_DATABASE_URL='%%DATABASE_MIGRATION_URL_LOCAL%%'
cargo run -- setup --tenant acme --email owner@example.com --organization "Acme Inc"
unset FORGE_MIGRATION_DATABASE_URL
export FORGE_DATABASE_URL='%%DATABASE_RUNTIME_URL_LOCAL%%'
cargo run
# open http://127.0.0.1:3000/session/new?tenant=acme
```

`setup` asks for the password twice with echo off (or reads one line with
`--password-stdin`, or `FORGE_SETUP_PASSWORD`; never an argument), needs at
least 12 characters, and refuses once a tenant exists. The owner gets the
`owner` role; tenant-scoped scaffolds grant it their permissions and send
signed-out browsers to the sign-in page.

### Password-reset emails

Reset links are emailed once outgoing mail is configured; until then the
reset page answers the same and delivers nothing. In development, write the
emails to `.eml` files:

```bash
export FORGE_MAIL_URL=file:tmp/mail
export FORGE_MAIL_FROM="%%APP_NAME%% <no-reply@example.com>"
export FORGE_AUTH_PASSWORD_RESET_URL=http://localhost:3000/passwords/edit
cargo run
# request a reset at http://127.0.0.1:3000/passwords/new?tenant=acme,
# then open the link in the newest tmp/mail/*.eml
```

Preview the email in the browser without sending anything (development and
test only):

```bash
FORGE_MAIL_PREVIEW=true cargo run
# open http://127.0.0.1:3000/_mail
```

In production use your SMTP server: STARTTLS on 587 with `smtp://`, implicit
TLS on 465 with `smtps://` (percent-encode `@`, `:` and `/` in the password).
Certificates are always verified; a private relay's CA goes in
`FORGE_MAIL_TLS_CA_FILE`.

```bash
export FORGE_MAIL_URL='smtps://user:password@smtp.example.com:465'
export FORGE_MAIL_FROM="%%APP_NAME%% <no-reply@example.com>"
export FORGE_AUTH_PASSWORD_RESET_URL=https://app.example.com/passwords/edit
```

`src/bootstrap/mod.rs` wires `forge::mail::password_reset_notifier` from
these variables; `FORGE_MAIL_URL` without `FORGE_AUTH_PASSWORD_RESET_URL`
stops the application at startup. Send your own email through the
`forge::mail::Sender` port that `forge::mail::from_config` builds, and test
it with `forge::mail::testing::Recorder` (see the Forge mail guide,
`docs/mail.md`).
%%end%%

